# f11 -- READ COMMITTED: the row moved to another key while T2
# waited.
#
# T1 erases R1 as a move does, its primary key changed, and
# commits. T2's write is refused with `updated`, as over any
# newer commit; readlatest then says `moved`, not absent, and so
# does the write taken again: the row lives on elsewhere, and T2
# must not take it for deleted (ROWKEY.md §2.3). A reader sees
# the move's tombstone as any other.


== pairing RR/RR == T1=RC T2=RC T3=RC
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 move R1 -> ok
@3 T2 write R1 2 blocks -> blocked
@4 T1 commit -> ok
@3 T2 write R1 2 blocks -> updated (resumed)
@5 check last T2 updated -> satisfied
@6 T2 readlatest R1 -> moved
@6 check last T2 moved -> satisfied
@7 T2 write R1 2 -> moved
@7 check last T2 moved -> satisfied
@8 T2 abort -> ok
@9 T3 begin -> ok
@10 T3 read R1 -> -
@10 check last T3 - -> satisfied
@11 T3 delete R1 -> moved
@11 check last T3 moved -> satisfied
@12 T3 commit -> ok
@13 check committed T1 T3 -> satisfied
@13 check row R1 - -> satisfied
